# Bound the ClearCore telemetry spool.
#
# Install to /etc/logrotate.d/rooted-telemetry (root:root, mode 644 — logrotate
# silently skips config files that are group/world writable).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# telemetry_ingest.py appends one JSON record per STATUS_UPDATE frame, and the
# ClearCore emits one per second (STATUS_INTERVAL_MS = 1000). At ~435 bytes per
# record that is ~37.6 MB/day, ~13.7 GB/year, and nothing truncates it. On a
# 16 GB card with the OS already resident that fills the disk in roughly eight
# months, at which point writes start failing and the machine misbehaves in
# confusing ways. Flash WEAR is not the concern here — that works out to
# centuries on this workload — the concern is purely running out of space.
#
# The file is not an archive. It exists only as a handoff between
# telemetry_ingest.py and Vector, both running on this Pi; once Vector has read
# a record and shipped it to AWS IoT, the bytes on disk have no further purpose.
# Rotating aggressively costs nothing.
#
# copytruncate is REQUIRED, not a style choice: Vector tails this file with
#   fingerprint.strategy = "device_and_inode"
# (see rooted-telemetry.toml). Renaming the file out from under Vector changes
# the inode and makes it re-read a "new" file from the beginning, duplicating
# events. copytruncate keeps the same inode and resets the length, which Vector
# handles by resetting its read offset.
#
# The tradeoff copytruncate carries: records appended between the copy and the
# truncate are lost. For a telemetry spool Vector has almost certainly already
# read, losing a few records at the rotation boundary is acceptable. Do not
# reuse this pattern for a file whose contents must not be lost.

/home/rooted/telemetry_log.jsonl {
    # Rotate on size, not on a calendar. Telemetry volume tracks machine
    # uptime rather than the date, so a daily rule would swing wildly.
    size 50M

    # Two generations is plenty — anything of value is already in AWS. These
    # exist only to debug a shipping failure that happened in the last day or
    # two, so they are kept compressed.
    rotate 2
    compress

    copytruncate

    # Don't fail the logrotate run if the machine hasn't produced telemetry yet
    # (fresh install, ClearCore unplugged, ingest service stopped).
    missingok
    notifempty
}
